Z||000000||a420kid||11-04-2001||08:07 PM||||hi im new here i just got a 85 4 banger ( i want a 88gt with a 350 but this will do for now)<BR>anyway i love this car. it was my buddies and he got sick of fixing it so he said if i can fix it i can have it. but i need some help.<BR>ok it ran fine then the battery stopped charging so we replaced the alt and the battery it ran ok for a day or two then when we were headed out of my house it started on fire. i hopped out and put it out to find out that the little red wire off the battery that goes to the fues melted and lit on fire. its not the big wire that i think goes to the starter. we tried three new wires and they all light up as soon as you try to start the car.<BR>i was hopping someone might have any idea what to try to do to fix this so i can drives my new little toy  <BR>thanks||||reg||11
Z||000001||Nashco||11-04-2001||08:50 PM||||You have a major short in your system somewhere.  A positive lead, probably a major one, is grounding out (positive wire touching metal somewhere on the car causing a direct positive-negative connection).  If you've never traced down wiring, this may potentially be an unsolvable mystery by yourself.  First follow the wires and just check to make sure you don't see any problems with the wiring.  Look for frayed wire, chaffed wire, cut/broken wire, loose wire ends, or pinches/kinks along the wiring loom.  Visually inspecting the wiring harness will catch the problem about 25% of the time.<P>If you can't see anything, DON'T try and connect another wire, and burn it up again.  If you use a larger wire than the one that is grounding out, you will fry the wiring harness....and replacing sections of it is no fun at all.  If you know how to use a multi-meter, you can trace the harness.  If you don't, find a friend or buck up and take it to a mechanic.  Good luck, and welcome to the forum <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||fastback87||11-04-2001||09:15 PM||||I will look tomorrow on my fiero and see if I can find something that might help you trace your problem.  <P>I am assuming that when that wire burns all accesories are off (A/C, radio, etc)<P>OK does that wire fray when you turn the key on or when you try to start it, or after it starts and runs for a sec??  The reason I ask is different things happen at different positions of the ignition switch.  In the on/acc position everything should pull whatever amps it is going to pull when it is running, except for a few minor things.  The alternator is the only major extra thing that runs when the engine is running as opposed to the key just being on.  Now when the key is in the start position every accesory is turned of like the radio, heater fan, cooling fan and stuff like that, and the starter gets all available amps.  If that wire burns only when trying to start it sounds like there is a "bigger" wire shorting to a major ground "engine, exhaust, frame".  If that is the case check the wires going to the starter and leaving the fuse block.  First the starter I used to have an 84 fiero 4cyl auto, and my car caught fire when the main positive wire going to the solinoid got melted to the exhaust, I caught it in time and no harm done.  There could be a situation like that leaving the alternator too if the big red wire leaving the alternator is touching something that gets hot like the heads, block or exhaust.  You might also check and see if there is a ground wire in the wrong spot like in the fuse block.  I hope this is helpful and not too confusing.||||reg||3
Z||000003||a420kid||11-04-2001||09:35 PM||||ok well to try and help its a 5speed ive looked down the wiring harness and cant see any visalbe damage i havent checked the wires to to starter and such that ill do soonly. thanx for the ideas i priced new wiring harness for 450$ from painless but i dont have much money at the moment for this project.<BR>oh yeah the wire burns when you are starting the car. <BR>thanx||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||11-04-2001||09:59 PM||||If the wire only burns when you're cranking the car over, attempting to start it, it's almost surely a bad connection.  You may want to take off all of you starter connections one at a time and make sure they're clean.  Use a wire brush to get a good clean connection between all of your stuff, and make sure that none of the wires on your starter are touching each other or hooked up to the wrong terminal.<P>You won't need a replacement harness, you just need to find the source and fix a little problem, almost surely.  Rarely is a wiring harness so bad that the entire thing needs replaced, in modern cars.<P>Take a good long look at all the wires, and clean all of the starter wires.  Let us know what goes on after that.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000005||maryjane||11-04-2001||10:02 PM||||Hmm, Your friend said if you could fix it, you could have it? (Why can't I run into a deal like that?) Does that mean if Nashco or<BR>Fastback87's reply's work, that they get the car?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Welcome to the forum. Sounds like it may be in the starter circuit itself, I think most of the accessories are out of the circuit when actually cranking the eng over.<BR>Don<BR>edited to add this:<BR>you beat me to it, Nashco, by a few secs. Good advice, he can use the $ saved on mods<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||1
